{"name":"Golden Hind","hex":"#a37111","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of, or relating to, gold.","Having a color or other richness suggestive of gold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["(Can we verify this sense?) brassy drummer, brassy chub, etc. (Kyphosus vaigiensis), a fish found in southeast Asia.","Ellipsis of golden retriever."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden"},{"term":"hind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Located at the rear (most often said of animals' body parts).","Backward; to the rear."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A doe (female deer), especially a red deer at least two years old.","A spotted food fish of the genus Epinephelus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hind"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golden Hind","blocks":[{"text":"Golden Hind was a galleon captained by Francis Drake in his circumnavigation of the world between 1577 and 1580. She was originally known as Pelican, but Drake renamed her mid-voyage in 1578, in honour of his patron, Sir Christopher Hatton, whose crest was a golden hind. Hatton was one of the principal sponsors of Drake's world voyage. A full-sized, seaworthy reconstruction is in London, on the south bank of the Thames."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Francis-drake-galleon-southwark-london-uk.jpg/330px-Francis-drake-galleon-southwark-london-uk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Hind","wikidata":"Q546198"},{"title":"Golden Hind (passenger train)","blocks":[{"text":"The Golden Hind is a named passenger train operating between London Paddington and Penzance in the United Kingdom. The Golden Hind was first introduced in the summer of 1964; the launch broke the then record for the time of a Plymouth to London journey. The train encouraged one commuter to buy the first ever First Class annual season from Taunton to London Paddington."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Penzance_railway_station_photo-survey_%2837%29_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1547527.jpg/330px-Penzance_railway_station_photo-survey_%2837%29_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1547527.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Hind_(passenger_train)","wikidata":"Q48804782"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/The_Golden_Hind_Museum_Ship%2C_Brixham_Harbour%2C_Devon%2C_taken_1968_-_geograph.org.uk_-_738581.jpg/500px-The_Golden_Hind_Museum_Ship%2C_Brixham_Harbour%2C_Devon%2C_taken_1968_-_geograph.org.uk_-_738581.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/The_Golden_Hind_Museum_Ship%2C_Brixham_Harbour%2C_Devon%2C_taken_1968_-_geograph.org.uk_-_738581.jpg/500px-The_Golden_Hind_Museum_Ship%2C_Brixham_Harbour%2C_Devon%2C_taken_1968_-_geograph.org.uk_-_738581.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Golden_Hind_Museum_Ship%2C_Brixham_Harbour%2C_Devon%2C_taken_1968_-_geograph.org.uk_-_738581.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Brixham.hind.750pix.jpg/500px-Brixham.hind.750pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Brixham.hind.750pix.jpg/500px-Brixham.hind.75